WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:09.220
We have seen the amazing property of carbon
to form so many bonds with a

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:14.340
variety of other elements and even with other
carbon atoms. Its capability to

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:19.550
form so many bonds is what makes carbon widely
present. However, the living

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:24.940
world is dominated by one major combination.
What do we mean by this?

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:29.850
Well, although carbon forms numerous bonds
with several elements, the most

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:35.260
preferred element for combination is HYDROGEN!
Yes! Hydrogen and Carbon

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:40.329
readily form bonds to form a vast range of
compounds. Any idea what the

00:00:40.329 --> 00:00:46.670
combination is called? These are collectively
called as Hydrocarbons. As the

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:51.980
name says, they’re compounds chiefly containing
Hydrogen and Carbon. So are

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.260
these compounds only made up of these two
elements?

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:00.579
Not necessarily! Hydrocarbons mainly have
these two. However, the other

00:01:00.579 --> 00:01:06.080
elements like oxygen, nitrogen, sulphur and
many others also participate in the

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:11.119
formation of hydrocarbons. But the backbone
consists of carbon and hydrogen

00:01:11.119 --> 00:01:15.229
atoms.
So a hydrocarbon will chiefly have carbon

00:01:15.229 --> 00:01:20.140
atoms bonded to each other and
hydrogen atoms attached to them. They would

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:21.789
appear somewhat like this!

00:01:21.789 --> 00:01:26.690
Wait a second… these are the saturated and
unsaturated compounds of

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:33.560
carbon right? Absolutely!
That is why they are referred to as the SATURATED

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.890
hydrocarbons, those with a
single covalent bond between all the carbon

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:42.789
atoms. We can see that all these
are single covalent bonds here.

00:01:42.789 --> 00:01:47.170
And the others are called as UNSATURATED hydrocarbons,
that is those having

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:52.310
at least one double or triple covalent bonds
between the carbon atoms. Here

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:57.280
we can see one double covalent bond between
two carbon atoms! Hence

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:01.200
unsaturated.
Now in order to simplify our understanding

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:07.810
and to study these hydrocarbons in
detail, we divide these into three major categories.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:11.100
These are called the Alkanes, Alkenes and
Alkynes.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:17.930
Let us understand each type in detail. The
first one is Alkanes. This family is

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:23.090
that of SATURATED HYDROCARBONS. That means
all the carbon atoms share

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:27.579
a single covalent bond with each other. Now
can you tell me the maximum

00:02:27.579 --> 00:02:32.780
number of carbon atoms that can be present
in alkanes? Well, we cannot

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:37.780
estimate it because the carbon chains can
be too long to count. So how do we

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:42.540
study the higher alkanes? I mean the alkanes
which have a very high number

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:48.300
of carbon atoms? Well, in order to study the
alkanes collectively, we have a

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:55.600
formula to represent them.
The formula for alkanes is C n H 2n+2 . In

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:01.469
this “n” will be the number of carbon
atoms. So can we represent all the alkanes

00:03:01.469 --> 00:03:04.110
using this formula? Absolutely!
Let’s try it out.

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:06.849
C n H 2n+2
n  Number of carbon atoms

00:03:06.849 --> 00:03:11.689
The simplest alkane that we know is methane.
Can you tell me its molecular

00:03:11.689 --> 00:03:18.579
structure? Yes! It’s somewhat like this.
So here, we have one carbon and four hydrogen

00:03:18.579 --> 00:03:22.620
atoms. Its formula is C H four.
Methane  CH 4

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:27.879
It’s an alkane with one carbon atom! Now
let’s see if this fits into the formula

00:03:27.879 --> 00:03:32.909
for Alkanes!
What will the value of ‘n’ be here? ‘n’

00:03:32.909 --> 00:03:38.170
as we know is the number of carbon
atoms! It’ll be one! Let us substitute the

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:44.810
value of ‘n’ in the formula.
The formula is C n H 2n+2 . The number of

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:51.540
carbon atoms present is ‘n’. It’s one!
What about the number of hydrogen atoms then?

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:57.840
It’s ‘two times n’ plus two.
On solving it further, we get the answer as

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:04.340
4. It’s the number of hydrogen
atoms! So in methane, we have one carbon atom

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:11.340
and four hydrogen atoms.
Does that give us CH 4 ? Yes! That is the

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.591
formula for methane. The formula
worked fine for methane!

00:04:14.591 --> 00:04:15.591
C n H 2n+2
Number of carbon atoms = n = 1

00:04:15.591 --> 00:04:16.591
Number of hydrogen atoms = 2n + 2 = (2 x 1)
+ 2 = 2 + 2 = 4

00:04:16.591 --> 00:04:17.591
Methane  CH 4

00:04:17.591 --> 00:04:19.989
This is how all the alkanes can be generally
represented with the simple

00:04:19.989 --> 00:04:27.800
formula C n H 2n+2 .
Look at the formula! Do you notice something?

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:32.430
To get the formula of an
Alkane, all we need is the value of ‘n’.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:36.750
So we can easily find out the chemical
formula of the alkane when we know how many

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:38.660
carbon atoms make up the
chain!

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:44.960
Let us try this out as well. We know that
the alkane with two carbon atoms is

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:51.150
ethane. Two carbon atoms means the value of
“n” in the formula will be 2!

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:55.919
What about the number of hydrogen atoms?
The number of hydrogen atoms is ‘2 times

00:04:55.919 --> 00:05:03.800
n’ plus 2. That’ll be ‘two times two’
plus two. That is six hydrogen atoms. Thus,

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:06.250
the chemical formula of ethane
will be C 2 H 6 .

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:07.250
C n H 2n+2
Number of carbon atoms = n = 2

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:08.250
Number of hydrogen atoms = 2n + 2 = (2 x 2)
+ 2 = 4 + 2 = 6

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:12.150
Ethane  C 2 H 6
This is how all Alkanes can be represented.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:16.220
Now let us talk about the next category of
hydrocarbons in the unsaturated

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:23.879
type category. We will talk about Alkenes
in the next video!

